<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isis was a major goddess of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, revered as a protector of kingship, a healer and a model of devoted motherhood. Her cult grew in the Late and Ptolemaic periods, with great temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behbeit-el-hagar">Behbeit el-Hagar</a>, and spread across the Greek and Roman world. Her temple at Philae was among the last Egyptian temples in use, until its closure under Justinian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isis.jpg" alt="Isis" width="320" height="703" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isis.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
